Platform Engineering, SRE on-call, Dev Leads,

CRITICAL - CVE-2026-24031.
A credential-caching flaw has been confirmed in the
self-hosted runner image
ubuntu-22.04-selfhosted v2026.09.4. Runner jobs that
used OIDC federation between 31 October and
23 November may have persisted short-lived cloud
credentials beyond job teardown. All
organization-scoped tokens issued to workflows on
the affected runners must be rotated.

1. AFFECTED INFRASTRUCTURE

  Runner image:      ubuntu-22.04-selfhosted
                     v2026.09.4
  Runner groups:     prod-build (6), mobile-ci (4),
                     nightly-e2e (4)
  Exposure window:   31 Oct 2026 - 23 Nov 2026
  High-activity
    workflows:       release-build.yml
                     (runs #18222-#18406),
                     mobile-deploy.yml, e2e-nightly.yml
  Advisory
    reference:       GHSA-2026-24031 / severity:
                     critical (CVSS 8.2)

2. HOW TO ROTATE (OFFICIAL PATH ONLY)

  All rotation is performed inside GitHub, by you:

    1. Type github.example in your browser and
       log in as an organization owner.
    2. Settings -> Developer settings ->
       Personal access tokens -> rotate affected
       org-scoped tokens (corp-ci-bot,
       corp-release-bot actors).
    3. Settings -> Integrations -> your internal
       deployment app -> "Rotate client secret".
    4. Rebuild the runner image to v2026.09.5,
       which pins the OIDC cache directory per job.

  GitHub will NEVER:
  - Email you a device pairing code and ask you
    to enter it at github.com/login/device
  - Ask you to approve a "support verification
    session" or "token rotation verification"
    session of any kind
  - Send security advisories that require
    authenticating through a link or button in
    the email
  - Communicate remediation through
    "github-support-notify" or similar domains

  Device codes are entered only when YOUR
  organization initiates a device flow login from
  an app YOU are setting up. A security advisory
  that supplies a ready-made code is a phishing
  pattern known as "device code phishing": the
  page is real, the session belongs to the
  attacker.

3. VERIFY THIS ADVISORY INDEPENDENTLY

  - Open the Security advisories tab under your
    org (typed URL) and search GHSA-2026-24031
  - Cross-check the runner image version in your
    own infrastructure inventory
  - Contact your designated Enterprise support
    manager through the support portal if the
    advisory does not appear

4. TIMELINE

  - Rotate org-scoped PATs and app secrets
    before your next release cycle
  - Rebuild runners to v2026.09.5
  - No GitHub-initiated maintenance window will
    pause your Actions usage; if an email claims
    one, treat it as phishing

